st originally; one in their meld, we hold one, so two remain unseen. Not highly dangerous unless someone has a pung draw.\n- **Play W5**: Very dangerous. Across player has [W5, W6, W7], next player has [W3, W4, W5], and we have a W5. That\u2019s at least three W5s already accounted for (possibly more). Original count: 4 per tile. Unseen: W5:2. So only two left \u2014 if two are still in wall/hand, but given three W5s already exposed, playing this could feed a kong or pung.\n\nReason:  \nWe must avoid discarding tiles that can complete high-risk melds, especially pungs/kongs. B5 and W5 are involved in multiple existing melds \u2014 too hot. T8 and B2 are medium safety. W4 is acceptable, though we've discarded it before without incident, suggesting others don\u2019t need it. Best option is **Play B2**, as:\n- It avoids key numbers in active suits.\n- No player has shown interest in Bamboo 1-2-3 beyond early discards.\n- Our hand lacks support for keeping B2 (no B1/B3 except through melds now gone).\n- Preserves flexibility while reducing risk.\n\nAnswer: Play B2"}
